## What is proven

Under the supplied `MPY` semantics and the two named primitive contracts below,
`solution.py` is partially correct for every modeled list whose elements are
strings. Starting from the initial configuration, the exact translated
`odd_count` definition is loaded and called normally. It returns `ref(0)`, and
heap location `0` contains one output string per input string, in order.

For each input code sequence `CS`, the inserted number is

```text
digitOccurrences(CS, 49)
+ digitOccurrences(CS, 51)
+ digitOccurrences(CS, 53)
+ digitOccurrences(CS, 55)
+ digitOccurrences(CS, 57)
```

and that same decimal number is inserted into all four positions of
`"the number of odd elements ...n the str...ng ... of the ...nput."`.
On the prompt's domain (strings containing only decimal digits), this sum is
exactly the number of odd digits.

This is a partial-correctness result. `kprove` establishes the stated result if
execution terminates; it does not establish a separate liveness theorem.

## Formal claim

The program boundary in `SPEC.odd-count` is:

```k
#loadAll(Module(FuncDef("odd_count", Params(("lst", .ParamNames)), oddBody)))
~> Call(Name("odd_count"), (list(INPUT:ValSeq), .Exprs))
```

with `requires allStringValues(INPUT)`. The observable post-state is:

```k
<k> ref(0) </k>
<heap> 0 |-> list(oddRun(emptyOutput, INPUT)) </heap>
<heapLoc> 1 </heapLoc>
<stack> .List </stack>
<ret> noRet </ret>
<exc> NoExc </exc>
<exit-code> 0 </exit-code>
```

The module binding for `odd_count` is also constrained to the exact closure
body. Function-local `s` and `count` values are intentionally unobserved after
the loop; the return value, output heap object, control stack, exception, and
exit status are observed.

`SPEC.odd-loop` is the loop circularity. At a recurring loop head, it transforms
the output sequence `ACC` to `oddRun(ACC, INPUT)` while preserving the exact
environment, module binding, allocation state, call frame, exception, and exit
status. Its three obligations are:

- base: `.ValSeq` leaves `ACC` unchanged;
- step: one fixed-semantics iteration appends the sentence for the head and
  re-establishes the circularity for the tail;
- entry: module loading, binding, call setup, allocation, first iteration (when
  present), return, and frame pop establish the whole-program postcondition.

